WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.800
Part 2. You will hear a man called David talking on the radio about his work as a lifeboat

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:16.200
volunteer. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 16.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
You will hear a man called David talking on the radio about his work as a lifeboat volunteer.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 16.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
You will hear a man called David talking on the radio about his work as a lifeboat volunteer.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:01.000
You will hear a man called David talking on the radio about his work as a lifeboat volunteer.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 16.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
I never really planned to be a lifeboat volunteer when I came to live in North Sea. I'd been

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.500
working in London as a website designer, but although that was interesting, I didn't like

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.879
city life. I'd been really keen on boats as a teenager and I thought if I went to live

00:01:26.879 --> 00:01:32.760
by the sea I might be able to pursue that interest a bit more in my free time. Then

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:39.500
I found that the lifeboat institution was looking for volunteers, so I decided to apply.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:44.739
The lifeboat institution building here in North Sea is hard to miss. It's one of the

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:51.139
largest in the country. It was built 15 years ago, with funds provided by a generous member

00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:56.300
of the public who'd lived here all her life. As the lifeboat institution is a charity that

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.540
relies on that kind of donation, rather than funding provided by the government, that was

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:08.539
a huge help to us. When I applied, I had to have a health assessment. The doctors were

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:15.500
particularly interested in my vision. I used to be short-sighted, so I'd had to wear glasses,

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:21.380
but I'd had laser eye surgery two years earlier, so that was okay. They gave me tests for colour

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:28.380
blindness and they thought I might have a problem there, but it turned out I was okay.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.259
When the Coast Guard gets an alert, all the volunteers are contacted and rushed to the

00:02:33.259 --> 00:02:39.539
lifeboat station. Our target's to get there in five minutes. Then we try to get the boat

00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:45.860
off the dock and out to sea in another six to eight minutes. Our team's proud that we

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:52.860
usually achieve that. The average time across the country's eight and a half minutes.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.179
I've recently qualified as what's called a helmsman, which means I have the ultimate

00:02:58.179 --> 00:03:03.460
responsibility for the lifeboat. I have to check that the equipment we use is in working

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.899
order, the crew have special life jackets that can support up to four people in the

00:03:08.899 --> 00:03:15.300
water, and it's ultimately my decision whether it's safe to launch the boat. But it's very

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:22.220
rare not to launch it, even in the worst weather. As well as going out on the lifeboat, my work

00:03:22.259 --> 00:03:28.059
involves other things too. A lot of people underestimate how quickly conditions can change

00:03:28.059 --> 00:03:34.460
at sea, so I speak to youth groups and sailing clubs in the area about the sorts of problems

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:40.619
that sailors and swimmers can have if the weather suddenly gets bad. We also have a

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:46.660
lot of volunteers who organise activities to raise money for us, and we couldn't manage

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.699
without them.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.660
Now listen and answer questions 17 to 20.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:31.339
The training we get is a continuous process, focusing on technical competence and safe

00:04:31.339 --> 00:04:36.940
handling techniques, and it's given me the confidence to deal with extreme situations

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:43.359
without panicking. I was glad I'd done a first aid course before I started, as that's a big

00:04:43.359 --> 00:04:49.880
help with the casualty care activities we do. We've done a lot on how to deal with ropes

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:56.760
and tie knots, that's an essential skill. After a year I did a one-week residential

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.959
course led by specialists. They had a wave tank where they could create extreme weather

00:05:02.959 --> 00:05:08.679
conditions so we could get experience at what to do if the boat turned over in a storm at

00:05:08.679 --> 00:05:15.679
night for example. Since I started, I've had to deal with a range of emergency situations,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:23.399
but the work's hugely motivating. It's not just about saving lives. I've learned a lot

00:05:23.399 --> 00:05:29.760
about the technology involved. My background in IT's been useful here, and I can use my

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.519
expertise to help other volunteers. They're a great group. We're like a family really,

00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:42.600
which helps when you're dragging yourself out of bed on a cold, stormy night. But actually,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.559
it's the colder months that can be the most rewarding time. That's when the incidents

00:05:47.559 --> 00:05:54.119
tend to be more serious, and you realise that you can make a huge difference to the outcome.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:06:01.119
So if any of you listeners are interested, why don't you give us a call?

00:06:01.119 --> 00:06:09.119
That is the end of part two. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part two.

